l_normalized": true, "mention": "MAPK", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9527347087860107, "span": {"begin": 667, "end": 671}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:148022"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "TRIF", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9941439032554626, "span": {"begin": 720, "end": 724}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3661"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "IRF3", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9960159659385681, "span": {"begin": 743, "end": 747}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:11188", "5502"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "I", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.7197628021240234, "span": {"begin": 764, "end": 765}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3439"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "interferons", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.8327131271362305, "span": {"begin": 766, "end": 777}}], "text": "Cell damage activates inflammatory pathways through signals both inside and outside the cell. Under stress, breakdown of the mitochondrial membrane (MOMP) releases damage signals into the cell fluid such as mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) [1]and double-stranded RNA (dsRNA)[2, 3], and reducing protective proteins (IAPs)[4]. These released signals turn on major inflammation pathways. At the same time, the loss of IAPs activat,es NF-&kappa;B to produce inflammatory cytokines[5, 6]. Also, Toll-like receptors (TLRs) may recognize damage and pathogen signals&nbsp;through two main pathways: MyD88-dependent and TRIF-dependent[7]. The MyD88 pathway turns on NF-&kappa;B and MAPK to release inflammatory cytokines[8], while the TRIF pathway activates IRF3 to produce type I interferons. Together, these pathways work to strengthen and maintain the overall inflammatory response.", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:40:26 +0000 2026"}